course: dictionary
lesson: 877
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have lots of meanings, and the words around them tell you which one is meant. This is called context. «A promise cannot bind you to stand by while someone comes to harm.» Here bind means that a promise cannot force you to keep it when someone needs help. Keeping someone safe matters more.
explain: Bind can mean tie something tightly. A nurse may bind a cut with a bandage, and you may bind a parcel with string. The word bind can also mean hold things together. Flour and egg bind a cake mix, so it sticks together.
explain: Always try your meaning in the sentence. Swap it in and ask: does it still make sense? If you say «bind the parcel» means «tie the parcel», the sentence still works. If you say it means «sing the parcel», it does not.
fact: Bind is a verb: bind, binds, bound, binding.
fact: To bind means to tie something tightly with string, rope or cloth.
fact: To bind means to hold things together, or to make people feel close and united.
fact: «In a bind» is an informal phrase meaning in trouble or in a difficult spot.
fact: A «bound book» has its pages fixed together inside a cover.
exercise: «She used string to bind the parcel tightly.» What does bind mean here? | *To tie tightly | To count | To set free | To paint
exercise: «A promise cannot bind you to stand by while someone comes to harm.» What does bind mean here? | *To force you to keep to it | To write it in a book | To tie your hands with rope | To make it snow
exercise: Ella says, «I am in a bind; I left my bag on the bus.» What does in a bind mean? | *In trouble or a difficult situation | Happy and free | Reading a bound book | Tied up in a rope
test: «Flour and egg bind the cake mix.» What does bind mean here? | Make it burn | Wash it away | Blow it apart | *Hold the mix together
test: Which phrase means a boring or annoying task? | Bind together | *What a bind! | Bound book | Bound by the rules
exam: «The nurse used a bandage to bind the cut.» What does bind mean here? | To cut it open | To drop it on the floor | *To wrap tightly | To hide it from view
